  "image_name": "1d76cccb9353672cd57f11538b5aebac.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes a brain-wide circuit linking stress, emotion, memory, and motivated behavior. At the center is the ventral hippocampus, shown as a hub communicating bidirectionally with regions such as the medial septum, entorhinal cortex, and medial prefrontal cortex, while also interacting with the bed nucleus of the stria terminalis (BNST), basolateral amygdala (BLA), nucleus accumbens (NAc), and hypothalamus. The dorsal and ventral parts of the hippocampus are distinguished, with the ventral portion appearing more directly tied to emotional and stress-related outputs.\n\nThe colored labels indicate broad behavioral functions associated with these nodes: mPFC with anxiety, aversion, and goal-directed behavior; NAc with reward, pleasure, and motivation; BLA and entorhinal cortex with fear responses and fear learning; and BNST, hypothalamus, pituitary, and adrenal glands with defensive behavior, aversion, appetitive behavior, and neuroendocrine responses. The hypothalamus connects downstream to the pituitary and adrenal glands, representing the stress hormone axis, and glucocorticoids feed back toward the brain, including hippocampal-related circuits.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that the hippocampus, especially its ventral segment, integrates cognitive and contextual information with fear, reward, and endocrine stress systems to shape behavior.",
